@implements IDisposable
@inject TimeProvider Time
@inject IStatusRegion Region

@* THE WORKLIST: every published record, the ones that need a decision first, any of them reachable.

   A LIST OF DISCLOSURES, NOT A TABLE AND NOT A DIALOG. Each row's one control is a button that reveals the
   record's fields in a sibling region right after it. Focus never leaves the list, so after the 285th save he
   is where he was; there is no trap to build; and a form inside a table cell reads as cell content. One row
   is open at a time.

   ONE TAB STOP PER COLLAPSED ROW, and the button has NO aria-label: its accessible name is its own text, which
   reads the band, then the title, the year and the runtime. A label over visible text would be a Label in Name
   subject (SC 2.5.3), and the words are the same words either way. The band comes FIRST in the document and
   on screen, so he hears which band it is before the title and not after 285 of them.

   THE BAND WORDS ARE CORE'S. They come from ConfidenceBand.BadgeFor and are never written out in this
   project, in either case: a copy in lower case is how a count sentence ends up saying one thing beside a
   badge saying another, and a band renamed in Core would leave it lying.

   THE ONE POLITE REGION ON THIS PAGE IS THIS COMPONENT'S, for the debounced result count. Nothing else in the
   editor may write to it, because within it the latest message wins. No live-region attribute and no status
   or alert role appears anywhere in this file; the count sentence below is plain text. (Spelled in words on
   purpose: a test scans these files for the literal markers, and a comment quoting one fails like the
   attribute would.)

   NO PAGER: he finds records by title and not by position, so the search field, the band filter and the sort
   are the way through 285, and a pager would take the region away from the count. *@

<search class="worklist-search">
    <div class="worklist-field">
        <label for="worklist-query">Search titles and directors</label>
        <input id="worklist-query" type="search" enterkeyhint="search" value="@typed" @oninput="OnQueryInput" />
    </div>

    <fieldset>
        <legend>Show</legend>
        <div class="options">
            <label><input type="radio" name="worklist-band" checked="@(filter.Band == WorklistBand.NeedAttention)"
                          @onchange="() => SetBand(WorklistBand.NeedAttention)" /> Needs attention</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="worklist-band" checked="@(filter.Band == WorklistBand.Unmatched)"
                          @onchange="() => SetBand(WorklistBand.Unmatched)" /> @ConfidenceBand.BadgeFor(Confidence.Unmatched)</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="worklist-band" checked="@(filter.Band == WorklistBand.Uncertain)"
                          @onchange="() => SetBand(WorklistBand.Uncertain)" /> @ConfidenceBand.BadgeFor(Confidence.Uncertain)</label>
            <label><input type="radio" name="worklist-band" checked="@(filter.Band == WorklistBand.All)"
                          @onchange="() => SetBand(WorklistBand.All)" /> All records</label>
        </div>
    </fieldset>

    <div class="worklist-field">
        <label for="worklist-sort">Sort by</label>
        <select id="worklist-sort" value="@SortToken(filter.Sort)" @onchange="OnSort">
            <option value="@SortToken(WorklistSort.WorstFirst)">Worst match first</option>
            <option value="@SortToken(WorklistSort.Title)">Title</option>
        </select>
    </div>
</search>

@* Plain text, and the sentence is COMPOSED from the band words rather than written out. *@
<p class="worklist-counts">@CountsSentence</p>
<p class="worklist-shown">@ShownSentence</p>

<ul class="worklist" role="list">
    @foreach (var row in shown)
    {
        @* @key is the PUBLISHED key, never the TMDB id: the id is 0 on every unmatched record, and a duplicate
           key within one collection fails the render. *@
        var k = row.Movie.K;
        var open = k == openKey;

        <li @key="k">
            @* TWO BRANCHES, and the difference is the reference capture alone. Blazor applies a capture when the
               element is created and does not re-apply it to an element it keeps, so a toggle that has to be
               focused LATER cannot simply be handed a reference then: the one row whose key is the focus target
               is rendered with the capture, and every other row without it, which also means 285 references are
               never held for the sake of one. The target is not reset after the move -- resetting would swap the
               focused element for a fresh one and drop focus to the body.

               EVERYTHING ELSE ON THE BUTTON IS BUILT ONCE, in ToggleAttributes, and splatted onto both branches,
               so the two cannot drift: a literal aria-expanded, no aria-label, type button and the one tab stop
               are properties of that one set. A row only becomes the capturing one after a close, which is the
               state the owner is in constantly and the one a markup test renders last. *@
            @{ var toggle = ToggleAttributes(k, open); }
            @if (k == focusKey)
            {
                <button @attributes="toggle" @ref="focusSlot.Element">
                    @Face(row)
                </button>
            }
            else
            {
                <button @attributes="toggle">
                    @Face(row)
                </button>
            }

            @if (open)
            {
                @* A GROUP, not a landmark and not a dialog, and the role is load-bearing rather than decoration:
                   a bare div is role generic, ARIA PROHIBITS aria-labelledby on generic, and the name is
                   therefore computed and then dropped -- so the record's title would never be spoken on arrival,
                   which is the one thing A24 moves focus here to provide. "group" is the only naming-capable
                   container role that is not a landmark; a landmark would put one per open row into the landmark
                   list, which the stylesheet refuses on the same ground. tabindex of minus one makes it a focus
                   TARGET and not a stop, which is what keeps a collapsed row at one stop; the name is the row's
                   title, so the reader speaks the record's name as focus arrives and nothing needs announcing. *@
                <div id="@FieldsId(k)" class="worklist-panel" role="group" tabindex="-1" aria-labelledby="@TitleId(k)" @ref="panel">
                    @RowContent?.Invoke(row)
                    <button type="button" class="worklist-close" @onclick="() => CloseAsync(k)">Close this record</button>
                </div>
            }
        </li>
    }
</ul>

@code {
    // Filter at ~150 ms so the list feels immediate; announce at ~750 ms so a screen reader user hears one count
    // and not a stream. The gap is the point: the announcer restarts its settle delay on every call, so a
    // caller that spoke per keystroke would deliver nothing at all. The same figures the films page measured.
    /// <summary>
    /// The third badge word, which is the editor's own and not one of Core's band words: a record whose store
    /// entry asserts an id has been decided, and nothing in Core has a word for "decided, waiting for the next
    /// build" -- BadgeFor returns null for the confident band. See WorklistRow.Corrected for why the band
    /// itself cannot change until the catalogue is rebuilt.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string CorrectedBadge = "Corrected";

    internal const int FilterDebounceMs = 150;
    internal const int AnnounceDebounceMs = 750;

    /// <summary>Every published record. The worklist filters and orders these itself.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public IReadOnlyList<WorklistRow> Rows { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>The published key of the row to show open, or null for none. The component adopts a change to it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? OpenKey { get; set; }

    /// <summary>A row was opened. Carries its published key.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<string> Opened { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The open row was closed, by its own button, by Close, by another opening or by a filter change.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback Closed { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The search text or the band changed, after the debounce. Carries the whole next filter.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<WorklistFilter> FilterChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What goes inside an open row's region: the form, which is Task 5's. Empty until then.</summary>
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment<WorklistRow>? RowContent { get; set; }


    /// <summary>
    /// What is inside a collapsed row's button, in the order it is read: the thumbnail (no text, so nothing for
    /// a reader), then the band, the title, the year and the runtime. The band is FIRST, in the document and on
    /// screen, so the order is never split by CSS.
    /// </summary>
    private RenderFragment<WorklistRow> Face => row =>
        @<text>
            @if (row.Movie.P is { Length: > 0 } poster)
            {
                @* alt is empty because the title is visible text in this same row. width and height are the
                   intrinsic size of a w92 poster and reserve the box before the CDN answers. A record with no
                   poster gets a box with no image at all: an empty src can render broken-image chrome. *@
                <img class="worklist-thumb" src="@($"https://image.tmdb.org/t/p/w92{poster}")" alt=""
                     width="92" height="138" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            }
            else
            {
                <span class="worklist-thumb" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            }

            <span class="worklist-text">
                @if (row.Corrected)
                {
                    <span class="worklist-badge worklist-badge-corrected">@CorrectedBadge</span>
                }
                else if (row.Badge is { } badge)
                {
                    <span class="worklist-badge worklist-badge-@row.Band.ToString().ToLowerInvariant()">@badge</span>
                }
                <span class="worklist-title" id="@TitleId(row.Movie.K)">@row.Movie.T</span>
                <span class="worklist-meta">
                    @if (row.Movie.Y is { } year)
                    {
                        <span class="worklist-year">@year</span>
                    }
                    <span class="worklist-runtime">@FormatRuntime(row.Movie.Rt)</span>
                </span>
            </span>
        </text>;

    private sealed class RefSlot { public ElementReference Element; }

    private readonly RefSlot focusSlot = new();

    private WorklistFilter filter = WorklistFilter.Default;
    private string typed = string.Empty;
    private string? openKey;
    private string? seenOpenParameter;
    private IReadOnlyList<WorklistRow>? seenRows;
    private List<WorklistRow> shown = [];
    private string[] shownKeys = [];

    private ElementReference panel;
    // Every assignment to this must also set focusToggle; the reason is spelled out in OnAfterRenderAsync.
    private string? focusKey;
    private bool focusToggle;
    private bool focusPanel;
    private bool disposed;
    private ITimer? filterTimer;
    private ITimer? announceTimer;

    /// <summary>
    /// The toggle's whole attribute set, for both render branches. The state is the literal string "true" or
    /// "false" and is never omitted: a test helper exempts a dangling controls reference only on that literal.
    /// </summary>
    private Dictionary<string, object> ToggleAttributes(string key, bool open) => new()
    {
        ["type"] = "button",
        ["class"] = "worklist-row",
        ["aria-expanded"] = open ? "true" : "false",
        ["aria-controls"] = FieldsId(key),
        ["onclick"] = EventCallback.Factory.Create<MouseEventArgs>(this, () => ToggleAsync(key)),
    };

    private static string FieldsId(string key) => $"row-{key}-fields";

    private static string TitleId(string key) => $"row-{key}-title";

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        if (OpenKey != seenOpenParameter)
        {
            seenOpenParameter = OpenKey;
            openKey = OpenKey;
            focusPanel |= OpenKey is not null;
        }

        if (!ReferenceEquals(Rows, seenRows))
        {
            seenRows = Rows;
            Refilter();
        }
    }

    // ============================================================ what is shown

    private void Refilter()
    {
        var query = filter.Query.Trim();

        var rows = Rows.Where(r => filter.Band switch
        {
            WorklistBand.Unmatched => r.Band == Confidence.Unmatched,
            WorklistBand.Uncertain => r.Band == Confidence.Uncertain,
            WorklistBand.NeedAttention => r.Band != Confidence.Confident,
            _ => true,
        });

        if (query.Length > 0)
            rows = rows.Where(r =>
                r.Movie.T.Contains(query, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
                || r.Movie.Dir.Any(d => d.Contains(query, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)));

        shown = (filter.Sort == WorklistSort.Title
            ? rows.OrderBy(r => r.Movie.Srt ?? r.Movie.T, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)
            : rows.OrderBy(r => r.Band).ThenBy(r => r.Movie.Mc).ThenBy(r => r.Movie.Srt ?? r.Movie.T, StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
            .ThenBy(r => r.Movie.K, StringComparer.Ordinal)
            .ToList();

        shownKeys = shown.Select(r => r.Movie.K).ToArray();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// A corrected row is one fewer thing to do, and the sentence has to say so in the same render as the
    /// badge: the regions are visually hidden, so nothing spoken is ever a substitute for showing it. The row
    /// itself does NOT leave the filtered list -- the filter is still by band -- because in place keeps focus
    /// where he put it and needs no second announcement to explain where the row went.
    /// </summary>
    private int Attention(Func<WorklistRow, bool> which) => Rows.Count(r => !r.Corrected && which(r));

    private string CountsSentence =>
        $"{Attention(r => r.Band != Confidence.Confident):N0} records need attention — "
        + $"{Attention(r => r.Band == Confidence.Unmatched):N0} {ConfidenceBand.BadgeFor(Confidence.Unmatched)}, "
        + $"{Attention(r => r.Band == Confidence.Uncertain):N0} {ConfidenceBand.BadgeFor(Confidence.Uncertain)}";

    private string ShownSentence => $"Showing {shown.Count:N0} of {Rows.Count:N0} records.";

    internal static string MatchSentence(int count) => count switch
    {
        0 => "No records match these filters.",
        1 => "1 record matches.",
        var n => $"{n:N0} records match.",
    };

    /// <summary>A runtime as hours and minutes, with "2h" on the exact hour. A duration, so never grouped as a number.</summary>
    private static string FormatRuntime(int minutes)
    {
        var hours = minutes / 60;
        var rest = minutes % 60;

        if (hours == 0) return $"{rest}m";

        return rest == 0 ? $"{hours}h" : $"{hours}h {rest}m";
    }

    private static string SortToken(WorklistSort sort) => sort == WorklistSort.Title ? "title" : "worst";

    // ============================================================ opening and closing

    private async Task ToggleAsync(string key)
    {
        if (key == openKey)
        {
            // Focus is already on this row's own toggle, which is the button that was pressed.
            openKey = null;
            await Closed.InvokeAsync();
            return;
        }

        // Opening one row closes another, and the page is told once, as an open and not as a close then an open.
        openKey = key;
        focusPanel = true;
        await Opened.InvokeAsync(key);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Closes from inside the form, and puts focus on the row's own toggle: focus on a removed element falls to
    /// the body, and the next Tab would restart above the skip link.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task CloseAsync(string key)
    {
        openKey = null;
        focusKey = key;
        focusToggle = true;
        await Closed.InvokeAsync();
    }

    // ============================================================ the filter

    private void OnQueryInput(ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        typed = e.Value as string ?? string.Empty;

        filterTimer ??= Time.CreateTimer(
            _ => _ = InvokeAsync(ApplyTypedQuery), null, Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan, Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan);
        filterTimer.Change(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(FilterDebounceMs), Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan);
    }

    /// <remarks>Internal so a test can run it as a timer that had ALREADY queued it would after the page is gone.</remarks>
    internal async Task ApplyTypedQuery()
    {
        if (disposed) return;

        await ApplyAsync(filter with { Query = typed.Trim() });
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    private Task SetBand(WorklistBand band) => ApplyAsync(filter with { Band = band });

    private async Task OnSort(ChangeEventArgs e)
    {
        // Sorting removes nothing, so it does not close the open row and does not need announcing: the select
        // announces its own new value.
        filter = filter with { Sort = e.Value as string == "title" ? WorklistSort.Title : WorklistSort.WorstFirst };
        Refilter();
        await FilterChanged.InvokeAsync(filter);
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The one way the selection changes. A change closes the open row (correction 13): the debounce can
    /// filter that very row out and remove the form from under him. Focus needs no move, because the only route
    /// here is the search field or a radio, and whichever it was still holds focus; nothing needs announcing
    /// beyond the count.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ApplyAsync(WorklistFilter next)
    {
        if (next.Query == filter.Query && next.Band == filter.Band) return;

        filter = next;
        Refilter();

        var hadOpen = openKey is not null;
        openKey = null;

        ScheduleCountAnnouncement();
        await FilterChanged.InvokeAsync(filter);
        if (hadOpen) await Closed.InvokeAsync();
    }

    private void ScheduleCountAnnouncement()
    {
        announceTimer ??= Time.CreateTimer(
            _ => _ = InvokeAsync(AnnounceCount), null, Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan, Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan);
        announceTimer.Change(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(AnnounceDebounceMs), Timeout.InfiniteTimeSpan);
    }

    internal void AnnounceCount()
    {
        if (disposed) return;

        Region.Status(MatchSentence(shown.Count));
    }

    // ============================================================ focus

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (disposed) return;

        // Opening: the revealed region itself, once it exists. Nothing is announced, because a message that
        // receives focus is outside SC 4.1.3's scope, and the reader speaks the record's name as it arrives.
        // Only when that row is actually on screen: a key the filter hides has no region, and a reference to
        // an element that was never rendered is not one a focus call can use.
        //
        // The panel wins, and a toggle-focus still pending is DROPPED, not deferred. Left set, it would fire on
        // the NEXT render of any kind -- a keystroke in the search field is enough -- and pull focus out of
        // the field he is typing in.
        //
        // AS THE COMPONENT STANDS, THAT CLEAR IS UNREACHABLE, and it is kept on purpose. focusToggle is set in
        // one place, CloseAsync, which sets openKey to null in the same batch, so the branch below cannot be
        // the one taken while it is pending; focusPanel is set by ToggleAsync and by OnParametersSet, neither
        // of which can run between CloseAsync and its render. It is one new caller away from reachable -- any
        // path that opens a row while a close is pending -- and the line is what makes that caller safe rather
        // than a focus move into the row he just left. Do not delete it without re-reading the invariant below.
        //
        // THE INVARIANT THIS WHOLE MECHANISM RESTS ON: every change of focusKey destroys and recreates two
        // buttons (the old capturing row and the new one), which is harmless only because every path that
        // changes focusKey also sets focusToggle in the same batch. The one case where the destroyed button
        // held focus is straight after a Close, and that is always followed by a deliberate move. So any later
        // edit that sets focusKey WITHOUT focusToggle drops focus to <body>, and the next Tab restarts above the
        // skip link. Set them together or not at all.
        if (focusPanel && openKey is not null && Array.IndexOf(shownKeys, openKey) >= 0)
        {
            focusPanel = false;
            focusToggle = false;
            await TryFocus(panel);
        }
        else if (focusToggle)
        {
            focusToggle = false;
            await TryFocus(focusSlot.Element);
        }
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// Interop can fail, and an escape from here would reveal the host page's error bar, which carries an
    /// assertive role of its own on a page that has just done what the visitor asked.
    /// </remarks>
    private static async Task TryFocus(ElementReference element)
    {
        try
        {
            await element.FocusAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is JSException or TaskCanceledException or ObjectDisposedException)
        {
        }
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// Whoever writes a standing statement clears it. The announcer is a scoped singleton and the layout survives
    /// navigation, so a count left in the region would still be readable on the next page. Both timers first: one
    /// firing after this would write a count into a region that has just been cleared.
    /// </remarks>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        disposed = true;
        filterTimer?.Dispose();
        announceTimer?.Dispose();
        Region.Status("");
    }
}
